<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Taifa of Almería</h1><p class="st-meta">1145 CE – 1147 CE (approx.) · capital Almería</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Almería's brief independence in the 1140s, ended when a Castilian, Genoese and Catalan crusade stormed the great port in 1147.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1145 the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>, then the chief Andalusi port and naval base, rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>. Power passed to local leaders, notably the admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-maymun">Ibn Maymun</a>, whose family had commanded the Almoravid fleet.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> remained a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> weaving and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> trade, but also of corsair raiding against Christian shipping, which made it a target for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>. Its new rulers had little time to establish a stable government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In October 1147 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of Castile</a>, with Genoese, Pisan, Catalan and Navarrese forces, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> after a siege, creating <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/castilian-almeria">Castilian Almería</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1145 CE – 1147 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.49%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1145 CE – 1147 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Almería</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ibn Maymun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Almería of Ibn Maymun</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1146&amp;focus=second-taifa-of-almeria" aria-label="Second Taifa of Almería on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4618 850 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Second Taifa of Almería lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4938 1106L4799 1289M4938 1106L4938 1106"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4799 1289h0M4938 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4938 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4938 1106h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Almería. <a href="/atlas?year=1146&amp;focus=second-taifa-of-almeria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
